cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use bitwhiz, please cite the version you used."
title: "bitwhiz: a one-dimensional Lagrangian hydrocode for shock and detonation physics"
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Kittell
    given-names: David E.
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-7065-6780"
version: 1.4.0
doi: 10.5281/zenodo.23999297
date-released: 2026-11-07
license: Apache-2.0
repository-code: "https://github.com/kitte048/bitwhiz"
url: "https://nanohub.org/tools/bitwhiz"
abstract: >-
  bitwhiz is a one-dimensional, planar, Lagrangian hydrocode in Fortran 2018,
  the reference code for ME 697, Intermediate Shock and Detonation Theory
  (Purdue, Fall 2026). It has two solution methods in one code (artificial
  viscosity, and Godunov's method with an acoustic Riemann solver at first or
  second order), the Mie-Grüneisen equation of state with temperature, the
  History Variable Reactive Burn (HVRB) and ThermalCascade (gated by a
  lead-shock detector) with ideal-gas products,
  starting-velocity profiles, spall, tracers, a history file of the whole
  mesh, and pictures. Each method follows a frozen equation sheet, and the
  code is checked against 50-digit references, exact solutions and
  independent reference data. It is also a home for open source burn models,
  each written down in an equation sheet and checked in the open, and it runs
  in a browser as a nanoHUB tool.
keywords:
  - hydrocode
  - shock physics
  - detonation
  - reactive burn
  - HVRB
  - ThermalCascade
  - Mie-Grüneisen
  - Riemann solver
  - artificial viscosity
  - Fortran
